appening with Italy we thought that we  could convince the British public to  behave in a proper manner and they  didn't and we saw what happened at the  weekend the Sun came out they were  climbing Snowden I think that we've  followed advice at every stage on what  is the appropriate measure to take and I  think that's the right thing to do in a  democracy it's perfectly understandable  that people have a range of views and to  disagree judgments can be formed now  judgments will be formed in due course I  think the most important thing now  though is to do everything that we can  as individuals in our communities and in  government to do to follow the steps  which will limit the spread of this  disease how long do you think we'll be  in lockdown the prime minister said  three weeks during his address it wasn't  off-the-cuff you know it was scripted he  was reading it from an autocue three  weeks does that mean in three weeks time  we're all going to be okay we can go  back to work it means that we will  review in three weeks time exactly how  these measures are working let me be  clear this is a public health emergency  a national emergency we must do  everything that we can to stop this  virus spreading as the Prime Minister  said in his statement if the virus  spreads in an unconstrained way that  will overwhelm the health services of  any nation so that's why we're taking  the steps that we're taking that's why  it's vital that people follow them we  will keep it under review and in three  weeks time we'll be able to say whether  or not the the path that we followed  needs any further intensification or  whether there are volunteer of the  existing restrictions that may be  capable of being lifted but for the  moment the most important thing is that  people in the days ahead follow this  advice what is that three weeks look  like so over the next three weeks we're  going to see an increase daily on the  number of deaths is it going to we've  all seen the charts